    <DataNode elementId="SPERMINE" textLabel="spermine" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="PFNFFQXMRSDOHW-UHFFFAOYSA-R" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="650" centerY="640" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>The history of |FRAME: SPERMINE| biochemistry goes back more than 300 years. When Antoni van Leeuwenhoek looked at human semen through the lenses of his microscope in 1678, he noted the formation of crystals in aging sperm. More than 200 years later, the basic component of these phosphate crystals was named spermine, after the source |CITS: [11537482]|.</Comment>
